-- title:
--   ̄ F_ℓ is finite over ℚ̄(j)
-- statement:
--   Let $\ell$ be a prime. Write $\bar{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` and let $\bar F_\ell =$ `modularFunctionFieldBar ℓ` be the intermediate field of $\bar{\mathbb{Q}} \subseteq \bar{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ obtained as the $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$-subfield generated by the image, under the coefficientwise embedding `coeffEmb` of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ into $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ induced by $\mathbb{Q} \to \bar{\mathbb{Q}}$, of the field `modularFunctionFieldFull ℓ`, the latter being the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the set `divisorExpansions ℓ`. Inside $\bar F_\ell$ consider the element $\bar j$ given by `coeffEmb` applied to the Laurent series `jq` $= q^{-1} \cdot$ `jNumQ`, the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$ with rational coefficients (its membership in $\bar F_\ell$ coming from `coeffEmb_mem_laurentBaseChange` together with `jq_mem_full ℓ`), and let $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}(\bar j)$ be the intermediate field of $\bar{\mathbb{Q}} \subseteq \bar F_\ell$ generated by the singleton $\{\bar j\}$. The assertion is that $\bar F_\ell$ is finite-dimensional as a vector space over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}(\bar j)$, i.e. $[\bar F_\ell : \bar{\mathbb{Q}}(\bar j)] < \infty$.
--
--   This is the statement that the function field of the modular curve $X_0(\ell)$ over $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$, realised concretely as a field of $q$-expansions, is a finite extension of the rational function field $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}(j)$, so that $X_0(\ell)$ is a finite cover of the $j$-line. It supplies the finiteness hypothesis needed in the valuation-theoretic study of places of $\bar F_\ell$, and is used in the identification of places attached to roots of the modular polynomial and in the analysis of principal multiples of the cuspidal divisor.
